`env/env_0071_nonstandard_auto_insurer` is delivered: all three delivery conditions hold. Every difficulty passed its source audit and its KB audit, and the whole scenario loads `VALID`.

**What's in it.** The company is Cardinal Point Insurance Company, a nonstandard auto insurer writing in FL, VA, TX, GA, AZ and OH. There are 5 difficulties with 2 tasks each, and 7 published products with 332 KB documents in total.

| Difficulty | Product | Policy-audit rounds | KB audit |
|---|---|---|---|
| installment_billing_and_fees | billing (58 docs) | 20 | PASS, no open items |
| sr22_fr44_filings | fr (54 docs) | 9 | PASS, no open items |
| cancellation_reinstatement_and_exclusions | auto (74 docs) | 14 | PASS, no open items |
| physical_damage_claims | physdam (56 docs) | 6 | PASS; one wording item (S2) deliberately not fixed |
| nonowner_policies_and_renewals | nonowner (63 docs) | 10 | PASS, no open items |

The rental (14 docs) and towing (13 docs) products belong to no difficulty. They passed the render check but, as the design specifies, nobody audited them.

**Final checks, re-run by me:**
- Each difficulty passes all 7 of its checks, and the published-docs traceability check is clean.
- All 7 products pass the document check.
- No phone, license, name or other identifier is shared between difficulties.
- The blueprint has not changed since it was frozen.

**Judgement calls you may want to review** (each is logged with its reasoning in `worklog.md`):
- **Realism rules.** Reworkers kept replacing rejected factors with invented company add-ons. I wrote one shared set of rules for both reworkers and auditors (points 23–27 in `audits/render_source/XF_recipe.md`), and that stopped it. For example, prices hang off the premium-rating plan, statutory figures must be the real ones, and flat fees like NSF or SR-22 filing fees are not padded with made-up factors.
- **One accepted limitation in billing.** On a policy that backs two or more SR-22/FR-44 certificates, the invoice bills only the fee of the first certificate recorded. The rules say plainly that any further fee is referred to the filings desk. The calculation language can't add those fees up per policy, so this is stated rather than computed.
- **Cross-product timing.** In the cancellation and non-owner tasks, an agent could send an SR-26 (the notice to the state that coverage ended) mid-conversation, because the filings documents say it is owed at once. That would have changed the scored figures. Those tasks now read the certificate as it stood on each transaction's own effective date, or start from a history where no SR-26 is owed.
- **Known blueprint issues left as they are.** In physdam, one package setting ("lender paid up to the payoff") conflicts with the extended rule; its document now states the governing rule next to it. The rental and towing products have wording gaps. None of these affects a scored answer.

The billing difficulty took 20 rounds, mostly because each fix to its out-of-scope pricing exposed one new edge case. A full test grid over every scope combination, plus strictly bounded rounds, finally closed it. I saved that lesson, and the cross-product timing issue, to memory for future runs.

The full run history is in `env/env_0071_nonstandard_auto_insurer/worklog.md`, which ends with the `DELIVERED` line.
